# io.github.lucarickli.Sdk//45: the runtime (runtime/Containerfile) with a
# development environment on top, the runtime of the VS Code Flatpak
# (apps/io.github.lucarickli.Code), whose terminal is one: the C and C++
# toolchain with CMake, Ninja, Meson and the autotools, gdb and strace, git
# with LFS, the OpenSSH client, Perl, pip, the commands a terminal and its
# scripts expect, and the libraries VS Code links that the runtime lacks.
# What Flathub's Freedesktop Sdk gave that app's terminal before, but for
# valgrind, ccache and every library's headers and tools (below, with their
# cost). build/flatpak.sh makes it as it makes the runtime: /usr becomes
# the runtime's files, /etc their etc, the labels the rest. The test stage,
# last, is an app on it whose command is the test (test.sh). x86_64 only.
#
# A runtime of its own: a machine with an app on it downloads all of it,
# the runtime's files included, as flatpak shares nothing between two
# runtimes: 435 MB, 1.24 GB installed (its OCI image layout, measured November
# 2026 with Fedora 44; the runtime then: 261 MB, 761 MB; the build systems,
# CMake to gettext, are 33 MB and 94 MB of it).
#
# Its labels are the runtime's, which FROM carries over as it does any
# label (build/flatpak.sh reads the image's config): the
# environment and the GL extension point come with them, unchanged. Only
# the ID is its own; the branch, the same Fedora release, is named again,
# so that Renovate's Fedora PR moves it here too.

# FROM the runtime's image (the flatpak stage of runtime/Containerfile), the
# named context runtime (build/docker-bake.hcl), which a build without it
# fails to pull, as the apps' do.
FROM runtime AS flatpak

# The runtime's packages stay as they are, at the versions the runtime's
# image was built with (its weekly rebuild brings Fedora's updates to both).
# Without weak dependencies, a line for each group, as the RUN lists them:
# - C and C++ (also rustc's and cgo's linker), OpenSSL's headers and command;
# - CMake, Ninja and Meson, the autotools with libtool, bison, flex, gettext;
# - gdb and strace, for the app's devel feature (ptrace);
# - git with LFS, the OpenSSH client (the session's agent, ssh-auth), less;
# - the shell's tools that scripts and tools/test.sh expect (ps, file, ...);
# - a terminal's own: clear, reset, tput, infocmp, tic, hostname, zip, script;
# - Perl with shasum; python, pip and the headers pip builds C against;
# - sqlite3, cpio, bsdtar, wget, nano: what scripts and READMEs call;
# - NSS and libxkbfile, which VS Code links and the runtime lacks (ldd).
# Not here, though Flathub's Sdk had them, with their cost on top of these
# (dnf's, download / installed, Fedora 44, November 2026): valgrind, 4 / 29
# MiB; ccache, 1 / 2 MiB; every library's headers and tools.
# rpm installs their English files only: the runtime's image keeps the
# base image's %_install_langs (runtime/Containerfile). Then, from the same
# metadata (-C), the record of every security advisory dnf knew for the
# runtime's packages and these, which the scan before the push goes by, as
# for the runtime. Last, the runtime's rules again for what the packages
# added: no file in ld.so.conf.d (flatpak's ld.so.conf would leave it out)
# and ld.so.conf still empty, so that flatpak writes its own; no systemd
# units, no build-id links, no /var. The metadata first, retried, as for
# the runtime.
RUN for i in 1 2 3; do dnf -q makecache && break; [ "$i" != 3 ] || exit 1; sleep 30; done \
    && dnf -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False install \
        gcc gcc-c++ make pkgconf-pkg-config openssl-devel openssl \
        cmake ninja-build meson autoconf automake libtool bison flex gettext \
        gdb strace \
        git-core git-lfs openssh-clients less \
        jq which file procps-ng patch unzip \
        ncurses hostname zip util-linux-script \
        perl-interpreter perl-Digest-SHA python-unversioned-command python3-pip python3-devel \
        sqlite cpio bsdtar wget2-wget nano \
        nss libxkbfile \
    && dnf -q -C advisory list --security --all --repo=updates --json \
        > /usr/share/fedora-bootc/advisories-at-build.json \
    && dnf clean all \
    && test -z "$(ls -A /etc/ld.so.conf.d)" && test -f /etc/ld.so.conf && test ! -s /etc/ld.so.conf \
    && ldconfig \
    && rm -rf /usr/lib/systemd/system /usr/lib/systemd/user /usr/lib/systemd/*-preset \
        /usr/lib/.build-id /var

# --- what the image does not say: the flatpak.* labels ----------------------
# The SDK's ID; its branch, the runtime's (the Fedora release). The rest,
# the environment and the GL extension point, is the runtime's labels.
LABEL flatpak.id=io.github.lucarickli.Sdk \
      flatpak.branch=45

# --- the test: an app of its own on the SDK, FROM scratch so that none of
# the labels above apply to it, whose command is test.sh. flatpak.sh test
# runs it in its sandbox, on the SDK as every app sees it -------------------
FROM scratch AS test
COPY --chmod=0755 test.sh /app/bin/sdk-test
LABEL flatpak.id=io.github.lucarickli.Sdk.Test \
      flatpak.runtime=io.github.lucarickli.Sdk//45 \
      flatpak.command=sdk-test

# ptrace, for gdb and strace, as the VS Code app has it: flatpak's seccomp
# filter refuses it to an app without the devel feature.
LABEL flatpak.finish-args.devel=--allow=devel
